narrative:
## Building String Transformation Tools

String functions take text in one form and return it in another. Let's build a toolkit for common text operations.

### Shout and Whisper

```xiom
fn shout(text: Str) -> Str {
  return string.str_upper(text);
}

fn whisper(text: Str) -> Str {
  return string.str_lower(text);
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(shout("hello world"));   // HELLO WORLD
  io.println(whisper("HELLO WORLD")); // hello world
  return 0;
}
```

`string.str_upper()` and `string.str_lower()` do the heavy lifting — your functions just give them friendlier names.

### Counting Letters

```xiom
fn count_letters(text: Str) -> Int {
  return string.str_len(text);
}

fn is_long_password(password: Str) -> Bool {
  return string.str_len(password) >= 8;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(count_letters("butterfly"));  // 9
  io.println(is_long_password("abc123"));   // false
  return 0;
}
```

### Combining for a Bigger Function

```xiom
fn analyze_text(text: Str) {
  io.println("Original: " + text);
  io.println("Uppercase: " + shout(text));
  io.println("Lowercase: " + whisper(text));
  io.println("Length: ");
  io.println(count_letters(text));
}

fn main() -> Int {
  analyze_text("Hello XIOM!");
  return 0;
}
```

`analyze_text` calls all three string functions to produce a full text report. Each small function stays simple — `analyze_text` does the orchestration.

